{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"peridental-still-fieldmouse","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\n`tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py::test_no_src_module_over_the_hard_cap` FAILS on pristine `main`, and the CI **Module-size gate** fails with it. This is not a working-tree artifact:\n\n```\ngit cat-file -p HEAD:src/rebar/_config_schema.py | wc -l   ->  808     (cap 800)\n```\n\nBecause the gate runs on every change, **this blocks `Verified +1` for every lane**, not just the one that caused it.\n\n## Cause: two independently-green changes that only break when combined\nNeither change was over the cap when CI tested it. `main` is Rebase-If-Necessary, so Gerrit rebases at SUBMIT time, after CI ran — and a line-count cap is exactly the kind of constraint that survives a textual merge while being violated by the result.\n\nMeasured growth of `src/rebar/_config_schema.py`:\n\n| commit | LOC | change |\n|---|---|---|\n| before `2664fcb237` | 784 | — |\n| `2664fcb237` Gate the rich-text wire behind a cutover flag | 791 | +7 (`reconciler.rich_text_cutover`, epic `vigilant-erasable-pony`) |\n| `a147945aae` Bound and reap the DC renderer's pandoc | **800** | +9 (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, same epic) |\n| `d2dea317ec` Trigger compaction from the close, out of band | **808** | +8 (different lane) |\n\nSo the rich-text epic consumed the file's last 9 lines of headroom and landed exactly AT 800 — legal, and passing, but with zero margin. The compaction lane then added 8 more on top and crossed it. Each was green in isolation; the combination is red.\n\n## Why this is not just \"someone should trim 8 lines\"\nThe 800 cap is ABSOLUTE and LOCKED (`.github/module-size-limit.txt`; raising it needs an administrator override), and the module-size policy says to split only along call-graph seams that already exist, never mechanically to hit a number. `_config_schema.py` is a schema + coercion + section-table module that has been growing one config key at a time from several lanes at once, so shaving whichever comment landed last just resets the trap for the next key.\n\nTrimming only the rich-text epic's own footprint does NOT clear it: condensing its `dc_pandoc_timeout_s` comment from 8 lines to 2 frees ~6 and leaves the file at ~802 — still red. A durable fix needs either a real split of `_config_schema.py` along an existing seam, or a coordinated trim across both lanes.\n\n## Why it is being reported rather than unilaterally fixed\nA split touches a file two active lanes are both editing, so an uncoordinated fix would collide with in-flight work. Recording it here with the evidence so an owner can pick the approach.\n\n## Cheap systemic mitigation worth considering\nThe near-miss is invisible today: landing at exactly 800 looks identical to landing at 700. A WARNING band (say, >90% of the cap) on the module-size gate would have flagged `a147945aae` as consuming the last of the headroom, and the collision would have been anticipated rather than discovered by a red `main`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `git cat-file -p HEAD:src/rebar/_config_schema.py | wc -l` is at or under 800 on `main`.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passes on `main`, and the CI Module-size gate is green.\n- [ ] Any split follows an existing call-graph seam, creates no file under 100 LOC, and adds no new `path::symbol` keys to the complexity baseline (the ratchet rejects re-keying).\n- [ ] The chosen fix leaves headroom rather than landing at exactly 800 again.","id":"add6-6d88-74dc-4bca","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["module-size","ci","config"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"main is over the module-size cap: _config_schema.py at 808/800"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786652832005266001,"uuid":"712aa800-7988-448f-b6bd-5eb34d438d27"}